{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790375213,"nonce":"966P5zdch9FXngXIjixomyR0OEA=","title":"Lua: a module's exports and its LuaLS annotations, for an embedder","message":"An embedder that publishes a Lua module to other languages (caribou) needs the module's interface without running its chunk: a build step describes modules before anything the chunk requires exists, and running it there runs the module's top level.\n\nThe compiler's types already know the table a chunk returns. zyntax_lua::exports(source) reads it from the types a chunk `load` compiles gets, with nothing compiled or run: the table's string-keyed fields (metafields, `__`-prefixed, left out), each a function with its parameter names and `...`, a table (by index, since classes refer to themselves), or another value; which functions are methods (`self` first, as `:` declares); the tables whose metatable a table is (a class's instances); and whether stores under computed keys may add fields.\n\nLuaLS annotations (`---@class`, `---@field`, `---@param`, `---@return`, `---@type`) are registered by scope resolution with what each statement declares (Scopes::declared), and exports carry them: a function's declared signature, a table's class name and instance fields, a static field's type. An annotation naming a parameter the function lacks is an error from exports (Error::Annotation); compiling and running the chunk ignore annotations as Lua does.\n\nThe inference keeps the chunk's own returns (Inferred::chunk), which it computed and dropped before; nothing it infers changes.","files":null}]}